WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.200
Usually, the best horror films earn their
scares with gradually escalating tension.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.889
The following flicks, however, jump straight
into the scares before the audience even gets

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:10.410
settled in their seats.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.179
Many of them are among the scariest movies
ever made, and every one of them boasts opening

00:00:14.179 --> 00:00:18.020
sequences that are certain to have the faint
of heart nervously eyeing the exits.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.060
These are some of the most terrifying opening
scenes in horror history.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.060
It Follows

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.730
As one of the breakout horror film hits of
2014, everything about It Follows seemed designed

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.890
to keep the viewer permanently off-guard.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:34.670
Its methodical pace mirrored that of its "monster,"
a relentless pursuer who could appear as anyone

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.680
and could only be shaken off by passing the
curse to someone else.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.580
"See, everything's okay."

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.180
[Scream]

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.700
In its terrifying opening, we follow an obviously
traumatized young woman as she flees down

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.949
a street to the beach - and something else
is following her.

00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:57.850
We don't know what it is yet, but we know
she's scared to death, and by the end of the

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.760
scene, we know it was for good reason.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.239
Some viewers may have been put off by the
movie's ambiguous ending, but there was certainly

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:08.549
nothing ambiguous about its opening, a master
class in nerve-jangling terror.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:09.549
Split

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:14.460
M. Night Shyamalan was on top of the world
after the success of 1999's The Sixth Sense,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.880
and after a long rocky stretch, he returned
to form with 2016's Split.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.410
This psychological thriller is anchored by
James McAvoy as Kevin Crumb, a disturbed man

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:27.740
with 23 different personalities - plus an
additional one only known as the Beast.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.620
[Grunting]

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.420
The opening scene illustrates every teen's
worst nightmare, as a trip to the mall for

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.149
three teenage girls turns suddenly sinister
as Crumb nonchalantly takes his place in the

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:43.200
driver's seat.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:48.980
IT

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.080
Stephen King's IT is widely regarded as being
among the finest of his standalone novels,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.680
and its 1990 TV miniseries adaptation scared
the pants off of many a '90s child.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.400
"Bring your friends."

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.260
[Evil laughter]

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.090
The opening scene, in which young Georgie
encounters Pennywise while looking for his

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:15.090
paper boat in a storm drain, is familiar to
many - which makes the version offered by

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.560
the 2017 adaptation all the more shocking.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.280
Much of the film's pre-release speculation
centered on whether Bill Skarsgard would be

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.599
able to live up to Tim Curry's classic turn
as the evil clown monster, but that question

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:31.069
was answered definitively within seconds of
the appearance of those predatory eyes, floating

00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:33.250
in the darkness of the storm drain.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.849
It's an opening that would have been all too
easy to mess up - but instead, the sequence

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:41.000
established a vicious tone for one of the
best horror films of the year.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.120
Halloween

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.490
John Carpenter's 1978 masterpiece gave birth
to a thousand horror tropes.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:51.160
It was a film unlike anything fans of fright
flicks had ever seen - and that includes its

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.140
opening sequence, which put the viewer squarely
in the shoes of a maniac.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.300
The scene leads us through the halls of a
suburban home, from the point of view of an

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:03.540
unknown person who dons a clown mask, grabs
a huge kitchen knife, and uses it to stab

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.830
a teenage girl.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.739
The first-person point of view is only broken
at the scene's end, when the girl's parents

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:12.959
arrive home to find the murderer standing
on their front lawn - revealed to be the victim's

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.360
six-year old brother, Michael Myers.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:17.100
"Michael?"

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.900
Carpenter would go on to a long and distinguished
career of scaring the living daylights out

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:29.330
of audiences, which anyone brave enough to
sit through the first five minutes of his

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:32.220
breakout hit could have easily predicted.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.220
Cube

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:39.239
Released in 1997, Cube operated with a mind-bending
sci-fi premise that revealed itself gradually

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:41.480
after a stunningly effective cold open.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.829
A man awakens to find himself in a cube-shaped
room with several apparent points of exit,

00:03:45.829 --> 00:03:49.600
completely unaware of how he got there or
what he is supposed to do.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.880
After finding all exits to be essentially
identical, he picks one and goes through.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:54.840
And then…

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:08.480
The rest of the film features some similarly
violent scenes - but none of them blindsided

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:10.819
us quite so hard as the first.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:11.819
The Hitcher

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:15.200
A young man picks up a hitchhiker by the side
of the highway, in the middle of nowhere,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.670
on a dark and stormy night.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:20.900
"My mother told me never to do this.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:25.120
That's a pretty spooky premise on its own,
but 1986's The Hitcher quickly turns it into

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.310
something even more sinister with an opening
scene that left audiences with no doubts about

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.759
where the movie was going.

00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:36.320
"Because I cut off his legs and his arms and
his head…

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.430
And I'm gonna do the same to you."

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.980
This opening scene introduces Rutger Hauer's
John Ryder as an agent of pure mayhem, and

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:48.910
Hauer's performance makes his previous turn
as psychotic replicant Roy Batty in Blade

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:51.590
Runner look positively endearing by comparison.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.140
"Do you wanna know what happens to an eyeball
when it gets punctured?"

00:04:58.690 --> 00:04:59.680
Evil Dead

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:04.520
Horror film fans had plenty of reason to be
wary of 2013's soft reboot of Sam Raimi's

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.039
classic Evil Dead.

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:09.430
But those fans had no way of knowing at the
time that the project was in the hands of

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:14.070
a major talent: writer-slash-director Fede
Alvarez, who made no attempt to rehash what

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:18.460
had worked in the previous series, but brought
a wickedly inventive tone all his own instead.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.150
The film opens with a terrified young woman
being hunted down and captured in the woods

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:26.979
by her own family, who appear to be religious
fanatics bent on burning her alive.

00:05:26.979 --> 00:05:30.780
But as her father fumbles with the matches,
the woman's tearful pleas take a hard left

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.830
turn - and it suddenly becomes obvious that
the only terrible mistake this family made

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.780
was not getting that fire started a lot sooner.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.100
"I'll rip your soul out, daddy.

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:46.240
I'll rip your soul out!"

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:47.330
Thanks for watching!

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.979
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:51.980
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

